A||||3||watdge||Choices, choices... what's a young performance enthusiest to do?||||5||
Z||000000||watdge||09-30-2000||02:36 PM||||Hey hey everyone,<BR>  I'm new to the board, and, much like xfilesgirl1, I too have been looking into the purchace of my first car.  I live in the Mid US so I don't think I'll have to worrying about too much snow.. but who knows, we got about 16 inches last January.  Anyway, back to the matter at hand.  I've always loved to see and read up on performance cars.  Just their ability to take less horsepower than the older muscle cars, Mustang, Comet, and all those other good things while still out performing them.  Yes, I understand that weight now takes the effect on these certain abilities of performance, so, I chose a car that I thought would be well suited for what I wanted in a sports car...<P>  Mitsubishi 3000GT VR-4<P>  Hahahaha, not going to happen.  Primarily due to the nice $6000 per year insurance tag on it for me.  That proved that a 3000GT wouldn't be on my driveway for quite some time.  Through all of this, it has allowed me to open up the doors of new possibility.  Different cars began to prove reliable and worth my attention.  Thus, the introduction of the Fiero into my mind set.  Small, agile, powerful...*maybe*?, light.  All of the things that I had previously had been looking for.  hehe, no I know those of you who know of the 3000GT are probably thinking that the GT is neither light nor small, but hey, it's good enough.<P>  Back to what I was saying, once I began to check out the specs on the Fieros, I began to get more and more interested in the car.  Being an engineering student, I have always enjoyed trying to find new ways to improve stuff.  Thus this has had me to inquire about these small things...<P>  I plan to buy a fiero and....<BR>- V8 350 small block swap (fuel injected)<BR>- Lower the car (or larger rims, nothing crazily low, but enough for plenty turning strength)<BR>- Finally, I plan to put a 355 kit on the car... which also means that I'll probably be stretching the frame down the road some time.<P>  My major question for you all is, having all this planned down the road in that order, what is the best year model for the upgrades?  I know that the '88 has the best suspention, but I have also been told that since there are fewer made, the parts are harder to find.  Anyway, thanks a head for any info and such of the like. <P>------------------<BR>The young, Mechanical Engineering student,<BR>Jeremy<BR> <IMG SRC="http://venus.spaceports.com/~flagg/pictureforformum.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Seanh||09-30-2000||04:01 PM||||I am wanting to do somthing like that in the future after I am done rebuilding my 86GT. I plan on a 350SBC engine first, and the Off-road Fiero body kit from PISA next. I will be doing this on my 84 which is not running at the time. It is difficult to find some parts for the 88 models. If I were going at it, in your case I would choose an 87. You kind of get the best of both worlds. They had a bigger gas tank, all the major changes made started in 87. They changed some more things on the 88 model and only produced them for a year, hence the reason it may somtimes be difficult to find part, but with all the Fiero specialty shops and knowlege of all the people on this forum, you will probally be able to find just about anything you need. If it is the suspension and handling your concered with, there are many options available like sport suspension, coil-overs, things like that. Also keep in mind that the 88's did not have a steering dampener, and not place to put one. You can purchase these from Rodney on his website: <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>   Just an added expense if you are planning on larger rims.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000002||watdge||10-01-2000||01:25 PM||||Hey again,<BR>Thanks for the info Sean.  I had always seen the various types of Fieros out there just didn't know which was the best for what I wanted to do.  But, to clear a thing or two up:<P>I do plan on lowering the car, I've been reading the thread about changing the length of the pins on the balljoints on the A-arms (sounds very interesting)<P>And, being only 15 *16 next month* I plan to have this car to drive for quite some time.  I'm not sure when I'll put the kit on it... the more I see the fieros the more I like them as they are.  Just the main thing is I would like a car that looks and performs well.  The '87 GT looks good in my opinion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> and is well suited for my budget.<P>Thanks for the info and insight!<BR>(P.S. thanks for the web link as well!)<P>------------------<BR>The young, Mechanical Engineering student,<BR>Jeremy<BR> <IMG SRC="http://venus.spaceports.com/~flagg/pictureforformum.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Old Lar||10-01-2000||04:59 PM||||I would not recommend any 88 for the difficulty in locating suspension components.  Any year model 85-87 is good to work with,  The 84 only came with a 4 cylinder and you may have wiring problems using the existing harness.  You can rewire the whole car.  If you do a kit that needs stretching, get it done by a professional.<P>After you initial cost of a car to work with, you'll have engine cost lots of variables there, transmission variables there 4 speed, 5 speed, clutches, various automatics choices.<P>What I'm getting at is that all the choices ain't cheap.  You need to figure in your time and your skill.  Then once you get it all together and ready for the road, the insurance industry will eat you alive because you have a "performance"car.  Even us old guys pay an insurance premium for having a Fiero on the road.<P>How deep is your pocket?  You can easily spend $10G for a good quality rebuilt Fiero.  If you do it yourself, figure in your time in dollar amount and the cost will be the same, except you have gained a lot of knowlegde in the process.||||reg||1
